      "summary": "iRobot's lenders rely on assumptions about the company's cash flow generation, Amazon-related break‑fee proceeds, inventory and receivables management, avoidance of extra debt, and timely financial reporting to support the Term Loan's covenants and collateral protections.\n\nReviewer note: every assumption here is reconstructed from public filings. None is Carlyle's actual underwriting thesis, which is not public. One of six reconstructed assumptions (R01-A01, operating cash flow sufficiency) was withheld because neither of its quotations verified.\n\nReviewer note: R01-A05 concerns the borrowing base of iRobot's bank revolving credit facility described in the Q1 2023 Form 10-Q. That is a separate facility from the Carlyle term loan reviewed here, so R01-A05 is weak context rather than a belief this package relies on.\n\nReviewer note: R01-A03 ('no additional debt financing beyond existing facilities') is anchored to a Form 10-Q sentence that pre-dates the term loan. Read it as 'liquidity needs will be met without further borrowing after this facility'.\n\nSchedule 7.07 (Minimum Core Assets Test step-down amounts) is referenced by Section 7.07 but is not contained in the SEC-filed exhibit. Adjusted test amounts other than $250,000,000 and the $350,000,000 step-up cannot be determined from this packet.\n\nMonthly Compliance Certificates and Consolidated Core Assets calculations are delivered privately to the agent and are not public. Compliance with the Minimum Core Assets Test cannot be calculated from public filings.\n\nNo Carlyle underwriting memo, lender forecast, internal risk policy or private reporting stream is public. Every assumption in this case is reconstructed from public filings and is not the lender's actual thesis.",
