          "text": "P1 — monitoring and officer knowledge\nSynthetic incident record FR-2027-081, available August 10, 2027. Production monitoring shows that a queue-lock defect prevented customers from completing regulated approval workflows continuously from 8:30 a.m. until 2:30 p.m. New York time on August 9. The responsible chief technology officer learned at 9:15 a.m. that affected customers represented 12% of FluxRail's fiscal 2026 consolidated revenue, counted once. The six-hour interruption was unplanned, and no advance maintenance notice had been issued.\n\nP2 — lender delivery and follow-up\nThe designated lender reporting portal records receipt of the initial incident notice at 11:00 a.m. New York time on August 10. The notice includes the incident duration, affected workflows, known exposure, officer knowledge time, and mitigation. A root-cause report has been commissioned but has not yet been delivered as of the August 10 review. Preliminary triage identifies a runtime queue-lock defect, rather than an incompatible vendor endpoint; the production connectors and validated migration bridge remain supported.\n\nP3 — restoration and counterevidence\nThe service team restored processing at 2:30 p.m. on August 9 and cleared the affected approval queue. Audit reconciliation found no lost, altered, or incorrectly approved records. No written customer termination or nonrenewal notice has been received. These observations do not establish whether renewal behavior or implementation support costs will change. The incident evidence contains no new quarter-end debt, eligible cash, or trailing-four-quarter EBITDA data.",
