          "date": "2027-09-10",
          "status": "proposed",
          "summary": "Draft discussion only: require an incident-to-lender alert control, a verified reporting-threshold register tied to the effective agreement version, and an independently tested escalation process. The user may request information, retain the existing terms, or negotiate a further amendment. No revision, waiver, consent, or new interest rate has been adopted.\n\nThe smaller September incident was not routed for notice because the internal configuration retained superseded thresholds. The proposed control links reporting rules to the effective agreement and verifies actual lender delivery, while preserving the existing duration and revenue thresholds.\n\nNone. No waiver is proposed for the missed September incident notice; its history and current resolution must be assessed separately.",
          "changes": [
            "S2\n\nProposed controls: agreement-version threshold register, tested incident routing, named reporting owner and backup, and verified lender receipt. Existing 2-hour/5% thresholds remain.\n\nBefore: For incidents beginning on or after August 23, 2027, the Borrower shall record every production incident that prevents a customer from completing a regulated approval workflow for more than two consecutive hours. It shall notify the Lender within two Business Days after a responsible technology officer learns that such an incident affects customers comprising at least 5% of prior-fiscal-year revenue, and shall deliver a root-cause report within 15 calendar days after service restoration. The report must state duration, affected workflows, data-integrity impact, corrective action, and recurrence testing. Planned maintenance disclosed to affected customers at least five Business Days beforehand is excluded unless it exceeds the announced window by two hours. For each incident meeting the notice threshold, the Borrower shall also notify the Lender within two Business Days after a responsible technology officer identifies a material unresolved risk of recurrence, stating the basis for that conclusion and interim safeguards. Beginning on the first Friday that falls at least two Business Days after that risk is identified, and on each Friday thereafter, the Borrower shall deliver a status update by 5 p.m. until recurrence testing establishes closure; the agreement's Business Day rolling convention applies. An investigation or possible recurrence alone does not establish that a material unresolved recurrence risk has been identified. Incidents beginning before August 23, 2027 remain governed by S2 as previously effective. Failure of a recording, notice, report or update obligation uses resolution category R. An incident's occurrence does not by itself constitute failure of a reporting duty or an Event of Default.\n\nAfter: For incidents beginning on or after August 23, 2027, the Borrower shall record every production incident that prevents a customer from completing a regulated approval workflow for more than two consecutive hours. It shall notify the Lender within two Business Days after a responsible technology officer learns that such an incident affects customers comprising at least 5% of prior-fiscal-year revenue, and shall deliver a root-cause report within 15 calendar days after service restoration. The report must state duration, affected workflows, data-integrity impact, corrective action, and recurrence testing. Planned maintenance disclosed to affected customers at least five Business Days beforehand is excluded unless it exceeds the announced window by two hours. For each incident meeting the notice threshold, the Borrower shall also notify the Lender within two Business Days after a responsible technology officer identifies a material unresolved risk of recurrence, stating the basis for that conclusion and interim safeguards. Beginning on the first Friday that falls at least two Business Days after that risk is identified, and on each Friday thereafter, the Borrower shall deliver a status update by 5 p.m. until recurrence testing establishes closure; the agreement's Business Day rolling convention applies. An investigation or possible recurrence alone does not establish that a material unresolved recurrence risk has been identified. Incidents beginning before August 23, 2027 remain governed by S2 as previously effective. Failure of a recording, notice, report or update obligation uses resolution category R. An incident's occurrence does not by itself constitute failure of a reporting duty or an Event of Default. Beginning on the effective date expressly identified in an executed instrument adopting this additional paragraph, the Borrower shall maintain a reporting-threshold register identifying the effective agreement version, incident-duration threshold, affected-revenue threshold, responsible officer and designated lender portal. Within five Business Days after that effective date, the Borrower shall reconcile its incident-routing configuration to the register, independently test the complete notice-delivery process, and deliver the results to the Lender. Thereafter, the Borrower shall reconcile the register promptly upon each effective amendment and test the process monthly. Upon learning that an incident meets the operative notice threshold, the responsible technology officer shall cause the incident system to generate a notice draft and alert a designated primary reporting officer and backup. A human reporting officer shall verify affected revenue and submit the notice to the designated lender portal within the existing two-Business-Day deadline. The Borrower shall retain and reconcile the actual portal receipt; a generated alert, draft, internal database update or unsuccessful transmission is not lender delivery. If automated routing fails, the responsible officer shall use a manual notice process without extending the deadline. Failure of these additional administrative duties uses resolution category R. This additional paragraph does not extend or reset an existing incident deadline, waive historical noncompliance, change an Event of Default grace period, or authorize an automatic amendment."
          ]
